00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.380
Sound good?

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:45.090
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
Sounds awesome.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.048
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:02:46.048 --> 00:02:47.920
So let's get started.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.050
War Dragons.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.000
It's a great game.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:50.700
I've played it.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:51.719
I really enjoy it.

00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:53.760
Gustavo, do you want to
tell us a little bit more

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.590
about what the game's all about?

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.260
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Sure thing.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.910
So War Dragons is our highly
polished, 3D, real time

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:03.970
strategy mobile
game where you go

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.260
to collect and control
hundreds of dragons

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.410
to take out enemy bases.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:13.110
In short, you get to control
a lot of very awesome looking

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.720
dragons and blow
up a lot of stuff.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.910
It was Pocket Gem's
first 3D game.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:24.560
It was our first game to
have synchronous, real time,

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.250
multiplayer battles.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:30.070
And our first game to
come out like on day one

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:31.370
with a lot of social features.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:36.400
So features such as
guilds, multiple chatrooms,

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.250
leaderboards, wars,
and much more.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:42.200
So you can see that
in that respect

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.790
it was one game that put a lot
more pressure on our servers

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.372
than any other games
that we had before.

00:03:47.372 --> 00:03:48.580
MARK MANDEL: Thanks for that.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.920
So the first question
I have for you is,

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.042
like, why did you build
a multiplayer game?

00:03:53.042 --> 00:03:54.750
You could have built
a game that was just

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.849
on a mobile device,
no interconnectivity,

00:03:56.849 --> 00:03:58.390
but you decided to
build a game where

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.110
people could battle each other
and there was a backend and all

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:01.950
that sort of stuff.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:02.982
What was the deal there?

00:04:02.982 --> 00:04:05.440
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: So when we
started developing War Dragons

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:08.310
two years ago, we
saw an opportunity

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:11.850
to build something that nobody
has built before on mobile.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.370
So there were a lot of games
out there where you can battle

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.950
other people's bases, but
it was always asynchronous--

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:20.329
like somebody can attack your
base but you cannot defend

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:22.407
yourself as they
are attacking you.

00:04:22.407 --> 00:04:24.740
There was nothing like that
on the market at that point.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.500
And we saw that
opportunity and we

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.970
knew that we can do
something like that.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:31.920
We also saw the opportunity
to build something in 3D,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.010
which we also never did before.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:36.130
We saw that the market
was right and the devices

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:39.610
were right for that
at that moment.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:41.920
MARK MANDEL: Now hold
on just a second.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.946
Is that you over on the
right hand side there?

00:04:43.946 --> 00:04:45.070
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Oh, yeah.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:46.020
That third character.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.369
Thank you for recognizing it.

00:04:47.369 --> 00:04:48.910
Thank you for seeing
the resemblance.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:53.170
That is Lord Gustav,
inspired by me, obviously.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:55.240
I think you guys can
see the resemblance.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.540
If you guys play
War Dragons, you're

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:58.880
going to see Lord Gustav.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.940
I'm the main villain of the
game, obviously, as well.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.150
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.540
OK, so this game of
yours seems to have

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.540
been reasonably popular.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.498
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:05:07.498 --> 00:05:08.580
It's been really popular.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.230
This is a graph
to represent that.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:13.230
So this is the number
of battles per day

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.210
that we had since we launched.

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.725
So you see that
first beginning there

00:05:17.725 --> 00:05:19.050
is right before we launched.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:22.810
When we launched we get that big
spike getting a bunch of users.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.080
After a few months we
got featured by Apple,

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.520
so we get that other
big spike over there.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.690
That last spike that came
down was a special event

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.270
that we had where
people could battle

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.700
unlimited number of battles.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.190
There were no waiting time.

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:39.490
So people did a lot of
battles during that weekend.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:42.110
And the interesting
thing about this

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:46.470
is that with all those spikes,
with all that variation,

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.354
we never had any
meetings to say what's

00:05:50.354 --> 00:05:52.270
going to be the impacts
in our infrastructure,

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.610
and how are we going to
handle millions of users

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:57.520
when we've had none so far?

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.750
And our servers are
going to explode

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:01.830
and we're going to have
to run around like crazy

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.740
trying to fix our
infrastructure.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.300
We never had any of that.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:09.510
Whenever we had those spikes,
nobody had to call anybody else

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:12.850
and come fix our servers
because things are exploding.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:15.840
We never had any of that
throughout all those things.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.990
Our infrastructure just worked
throughout all this period

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.410
of time.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:23.460
Just so you guys have
an idea, this is, like,

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:27.120
War Dragons has been out
for roughly a year now.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.390
And these number of battles
add up to 180 million battles

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:32.480
already.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:36.749
So just so you have an
idea of the scale of this.

00:06:36.749 --> 00:06:37.921
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:06:37.921 --> 00:06:38.420
OK.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:41.160
So let's talk a little
bit about how it works.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.030
What's the backend
infrastructure look like

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:44.840
and what's it powered by?

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:47.090
I know when you and
I first got together,

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:49.880
we talked about how
the primary part

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.070
of the backend
infrastructure is powered

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:54.130
by App Engine and Datastore.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:55.936
Can you tell me about
this history there?

00:06:55.936 --> 00:06:57.560
How you came to use
these two products?

00:06:57.560 --> 00:06:59.842
The decision that drove
you to those things?

00:06:59.842 --> 00:07:00.800
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Sure.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.174
So this decision
was a little bit

00:07:02.174 --> 00:07:04.140
before my time at Pocket Gems.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.820
But basically the story
is that Pocket Gen

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:10.740
started as two guys had
an idea for a mobile game

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.450
and started building
that mobile game.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:13.020
Right?

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.478
So they were two
guys that knew how

00:07:14.478 --> 00:07:16.780
to build mobile games but
they didn't know anything

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:20.860
about server infrastructure,
even how to code for servers.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.090
And at some point they
figured we need servers.

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.090
We need servers to collect
data from our users.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:28.370
We need servers to make our
users do stuff together.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:32.200
So they figured that and they
started to look for a solution

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:33.300
to their problem.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.500
So they started
researching out there

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.950
for a solution to their problem.

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:38.940
And one of the things
that they bumped in to

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.180
was Google App Engine.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:41.920
There were the
first few tutorials

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.080
out there for Google App Engine.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:47.310
They started to do the
tutorials and try it out.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:48.670
It was free to try it out.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.760
They were also pretty
much broke at that time.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.176
They were spending
all their money

00:07:52.176 --> 00:07:54.620
in marketing for the game.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:57.439
But Google App Engine fit
in their requirements.

00:07:57.439 --> 00:07:58.230
It was easy to use.

00:07:58.230 --> 00:07:59.920
So they started
building with that.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:02.160
So it was kind of like
knee jerk reaction.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:05.800
But throughout the six
years of Pocket Gems,

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:08.490
we never had a doubt that
that was the right solution.

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:12.450
As I said, we never had trouble
with servers exploding, people

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:16.130
running around trying to fix
because our users couldn't

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:17.640
play the game.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.540
We never had a DevOps team.

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:23.090
Like we don't have
people around like 24/7

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:26.560
because things can explode and
you have to get to the office

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.410
and fix things.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:29.630
So it paid off to us.

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:31.980
Like we sometimes
revisit the decision

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:35.000
to see if there's something
better out there, but so far

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.130
nothing jumped out.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:37.628
It's been a good
solution for us.

00:08:37.628 --> 00:08:40.169
When we started War Dragons, we
didn't even think about that.

00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:42.380
That was not an
issue at that point.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:43.020
MARK MANDEL: So it's
kind of something

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:44.270
you kind of fell
into, but it's been

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.710
so consistent and easy to use
that you've just stayed with it

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:47.320
all the way through?

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:47.680
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.100
It was the right decision.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:50.840
Even though it was a
knee jerk reaction,

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:52.010
it was the right decision.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:52.680
Paid off.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:53.638
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:08:53.638 --> 00:08:55.579
So just to set like the
stable set of context

00:08:55.579 --> 00:08:57.370
so that we all have
the same understanding.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.703
Hands up the people here who
have worked with App Engine

00:08:59.703 --> 00:09:01.300
or who are familiar with it.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:01.800
OK.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.100
About half the room.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:05.290
How many people here
have used Data Store

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:06.440
or are familiar with it.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:06.940
OK.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.100
Again, about half the room.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:08.500
Great.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:09.140
That's perfect.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:11.098
So I'm just going to go
through some very quick

00:09:11.098 --> 00:09:13.790
introductory parts
for these products

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:17.050
so that we sort of have
the same understanding.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.040
These products are
what I like to refer

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.020
to as managed products.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.100
What does that
necessarily mean though?

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:26.660
What it means-- and I think to
me and to many other people--

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:29.277
is the very direct
opposite of this.

00:09:29.277 --> 00:09:31.610
I know I've done this in the
past-- I'm sure many of you

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:33.480
have too-- where
you're like, all right,

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:35.540
I think I'm going to
get this much load in,

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:38.770
so I think I'll need four web
servers, and maybe a database

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.990
for replication, and
maybe some other servers

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:42.440
to do some other things.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.810
And this might be physical,
this might be virtual,

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.839
but this is really
kind of no fun.

00:09:46.839 --> 00:09:48.130
You're managing your own scale.

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:50.220
You're managing
your own machines.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:51.780
It's not really great.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.072
When we talk about Google
Cloud Platform services

00:09:54.072 --> 00:09:56.530
and managed services, what we
really want to be able to say

00:09:56.530 --> 00:10:00.010
is, hey, Google Cloud Platform,
hey can you handle this for me?

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.400
And it turns around
and says, don't worry.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:03.820
I've got this.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:04.330
Yeah?

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.496
We don't have to worry about
backend infrastructure.

00:10:06.496 --> 00:10:08.070
It doesn't matter
what our scale is.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:10.240
It's going to handle
that whole thing for us.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:12.760
So what does that
actually look like?

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:16.470
So App Engine is one of
Google Cloud Platform's

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:19.240
primary platform as a service.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:20.540
So what does that really mean?

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:23.125
Essentially what that really
means is you write your code,

00:10:23.125 --> 00:10:25.550
it bundles it up for
you to deploy it,

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:27.140
and it pushes it
up into App Engine.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:31.859
And App Engine handles
those requests.

00:10:31.859 --> 00:10:34.150
It handles taking that code
and running it in the Cloud

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:36.080
for you so you don't
have to worry about it.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:39.109
It's generally a web based
application service system,

00:10:39.109 --> 00:10:40.650
but it works perfectly
well for games

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:42.190
like we're looking at here.

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:44.170
The big takeaway I want
you to look at here

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:46.177
is that App Engine will
auto scale for you.

00:10:46.177 --> 00:10:47.760
So it doesn't matter
how many requests

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:50.780
come in, it'll spin up as
many instances of your code

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:54.360
as is needed to run as much
requests are coming in.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.424
So super, super powerful.

00:10:55.424 --> 00:10:57.090
At the same time you
don't have to worry

00:10:57.090 --> 00:10:59.870
about patches or security or
any of that sort of really

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:00.960
annoying stuff.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.000
You just can rely on
Google services to do that.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.420
On top of that, because it's
a platform as a service,

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:10.679
it comes with a whole bunch of
other services along with it.

00:11:10.679 --> 00:11:13.220
We're going to talk about two
of those services here that are

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:16.220
used primarily in War Dragons.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:17.550
One is task queues.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:20.340
Task queues is a service
that comes with App Engine

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:22.385
to allow you to run
background processes.

00:11:22.385 --> 00:11:23.260
It's pretty powerful.

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:25.730
It allows you to back
them up and queue them up.

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:27.110
You can run them in parallel.

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:31.310
It'll have retry mechanisms if
that code fails for any reason.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.240
As well as that, we're
looking at memcache,

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:35.000
which I know you take
great advantage of,

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.190
which is a managed
distributed cache system.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:38.612
If you've heard
of memcache, it's

00:11:38.612 --> 00:11:40.070
been around for a
really long time.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:42.420
It's pretty much exactly
as you expect it to be.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:44.295
And there's a variety
of other services, too.

00:11:44.295 --> 00:11:47.350
But we're not going to worry
about those too much today.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:49.850
On the other side of things--
so rather on the request side,

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:51.280
but on the data
side-- we're going

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.600
to talk about Cloud
Datastore, as well.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:56.480
Now the great thing
about Cloud Datastore

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.900
is it does sharding and
replication for you.

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:01.990
But it's a NoSQL managed
Datastore, as well.

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:03.500
So it doesn't
matter how much data

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:06.030
you put inside
Cloud Datastore, you

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:08.870
don't have to worry about
adding like replicas or mirrors

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:10.242
or anything like that.

00:12:10.242 --> 00:12:11.700
And the big takeaway
here for this,

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:14.140
as well, is that it doesn't
matter how much data there

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:16.270
is in Datastore, your
read and write times

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:19.400
stay the same, which is really,
really, really powerful and so

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.650
important for gameplay
and making sure

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:24.070
your player experience is great.

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:24.642
OK.

00:12:24.642 --> 00:12:26.100
So we've talked
about the products.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:26.760
Great.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:27.330
Wonderful.

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.163
Let's get into the nitty
gritty and actually

00:12:29.163 --> 00:12:33.510
hear about how all the things
that power War Dragons work.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.310
So when we got
together, we talked

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:36.480
about a variety of stuff.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:38.154
We talked about
communication over REST,

00:12:38.154 --> 00:12:40.570
we talked about matchmaking,
we talked about leaderboards.

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:43.122
Gustavo, you want to take us
through some of those things?

00:12:43.122 --> 00:12:44.080
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Sure.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:44.580
OK.

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:47.900
So let's start
with REST and JSON.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:51.820
So all of our communication
with Google App Engine

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:56.300
happens with using REST
request, with JSON as the data

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:59.420
representation over HTTP.

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:01.830
So this is like
industry standard.

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:05.170
It works really well for
mobile because it's fast.

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:07.380
It's faster to render.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:09.320
It's faster to parse.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:10.460
It's very easy.

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:11.830
As I said-- industry standard.

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:14.890
So Google App Engine handles
that really, really well.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:18.280
So that's where we chose to
do all of our communication.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:19.920
We have some real
time communication

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:22.460
that we do over a
service called PubNub

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:23.862
because basically it was there.

00:13:23.862 --> 00:13:25.570
It was a service that
had that real time,

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.430
so we didn't want
to rebuild that.

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:30.420
But all the rest
of the operations

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:33.310
that happened in War Dragons,
all the reporting and all

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:35.630
the analytics that we have,
and all the relationship

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:41.630
between players, all that goes
through GAE using REST and JSON

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:43.080
all over the HTTP.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:47.480
That is also a great advantage
because HTTP works everywhere.

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:49.890
On mobile there's a lot
of problems with networks.

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:51.641
There's like crazy
proxies out there

00:13:51.641 --> 00:13:53.890
and you go to a coffee shop
and there's another proxy.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.610
And if you don't
use the HTTP you're

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:57.630
going to have a lot
of problems on mobile.

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:00.800
If you use HTTP with
REST and JSON it's easy.

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:01.910
It works everywhere.

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:03.800
MARK MANDEL: So there's
really no special source here.

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:05.633
There's nothing
particularly crazy going on.

00:14:05.633 --> 00:14:06.510
It's just basic REST?

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:06.940
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:07.440
Yeah.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.580
The communication
is over basic REST.

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:11.390
If We can see-- oh, sorry.

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:12.060
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:13.960
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Whoops.

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:16.900
We can see here, this is, again,
this is the number of requests

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:19.480
that we had right--
this is 24 hours when

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:20.660
we launched the game.

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:23.520
So you can see the number
of requests going up there.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:25.330
A number of errors don't go up.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.170
Like our error numbers
are pretty low.

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:28.750
Almost zero over there.

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:30.730
And, again, we didn't
have to do anything.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:34.310
We didn't have to keep an
eye out for our servers

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:37.460
and see if we're going to
start to have more latency

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:38.760
or anything like that.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:39.500
It just works.

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.370
Whenever something
like that grows,

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:43.020
you can see the
number of instances

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:45.660
that your game has
it's being served

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.470
by the number of instances
that Google spins up for you.

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:51.666
They go up as well and your
time remained constant.

00:14:51.666 --> 00:14:53.790
MARK MANDEL: So you didn't
have to like rearchitect

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:56.220
your app as soon as you got
really popular or anything

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:56.720
like that.

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:58.840
It was all pretty much
easy enough to scale?

00:14:58.840 --> 00:14:59.870
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
Pretty much easy enough.

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:01.450
We had some things
that we're going

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:03.940
to see later that we
faced, but nothing

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:08.680
that was like user facing or
that broke the game in anyway.

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:09.890
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:11.460
OK, cool.

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:14.790
So I know you do some
particular interesting things

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:16.760
with App Engine and versions.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:18.356
For those of you
who aren't aware,

00:15:18.356 --> 00:15:19.980
one of the nice things
about App Engine

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.670
because its platform
is a service,

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:24.510
we take your code
when you deploy it

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:28.650
and we actually deploy it as
a version up into the Cloud.

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:31.870
This actually normally ties
directly to a unique URL,

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:32.480
as well.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:33.980
This is nice for
testing, if you're

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:35.290
doing, say, smoke testing.

00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:37.010
But in a web
application context,

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:39.654
normally you just send it
all to a default version,

00:15:39.654 --> 00:15:41.820
which becomes like your
canonical or your production

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:42.600
version.

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:44.180
But I know, Gustavo, you
do actually something

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.180
really interesting here.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:46.430
Can you explain that?

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:46.600
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:48.391
So we do something
different with versions.

00:15:48.391 --> 00:15:52.340
So versions is something that
usually people use for testing.

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:55.010
So if you're doing a
new feature or if you're

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.820
changing your website, you
spin up a different version

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:00.400
and you test on that version
and your production app

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:01.750
is still the same.

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:03.430
If you have multiple
developers working,

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:05.330
every developer can
have their own version

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:07.550
so nobody is stepping
on anybody's toes.

00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:10.480
But one of the users that
we got from versions,

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:13.960
which is a little
bit different, is

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:16.210
so that we are able
to break compatibility

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:20.830
between our client side code
and our server side code.

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:22.590
So how do we do that?

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:26.080
So let's say in this example
here I have my client version

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:31.510
2.07 deployed and people
are using that version.

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:33.811
And so for that
version, we deploy

00:16:33.811 --> 00:16:36.310
a version of Google App Engine
that is compatible with that.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:39.190
So the Google App
Engine version 2.07.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:43.130
As soon as we submit another
version of the client code

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:45.270
that breaks
compatibility, what we do

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:48.290
is we deploy another
version of Google App

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:51.730
Engine that is compatible
now with that version.

00:16:51.730 --> 00:16:54.680
And because the clients
have different URLs,

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:57.340
they each communicate
with their own version.

00:16:57.340 --> 00:16:59.810
So, for a brief period
of time, whenever

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:03.780
we release a new version,
we do have clients playing

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:05.280
in two versions of the game.

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:07.429
So if we had only
one server out there,

00:17:07.429 --> 00:17:08.470
we would break every one.

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:11.250
We would need to
have crazy code that

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:13.430
handles like, if it's
this version, do this.

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:14.680
If it's this version, do that.

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:18.099
And we all know how
ugly that can be.

00:17:18.099 --> 00:17:20.880
With this scheme that we use, we
don't have to worry about that.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:23.619
We can just break that
compatibility right out

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:25.099
and it all works.

00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:26.730
So then we have
those two versions

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.520
as people are upgrading.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.840
At some point we force
people to upgrade.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:33.650
And then what we
do is we basically

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:37.360
just take down that 2.07
version of the game.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:42.190
As we do that, that URL
doesn't see that version.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:45.100
So Google just redirects
you to the default version.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.220
And the default version
is always the new one.

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.380
And then now all
that the client is

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.130
going to get is
that please upgrade,

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:53.100
your game is not
compatible anymore.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:54.070
You need a new version.

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:57.810
But by that time everybody can
upgrade and then everybody goes

00:17:57.810 --> 00:17:59.740
go to version 2.10.

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:00.750
MARK MANDEL: Great.

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.042
So I know matchmaking is
an interesting problem.

00:18:03.042 --> 00:18:05.500
So if you aren't familiar with
games-- I'm sure many of you

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:07.700
are-- matchmaking is
basically just trying

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:10.660
to take two players from a pool
and pair them together so they

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:12.034
can have a game together.

00:18:12.034 --> 00:18:13.450
I know it's an
interesting problem

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:16.095
because you don't want like
drastically outmatched players.

00:18:16.095 --> 00:18:17.470
You don't want
someone to come in

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:19.240
and to be like
completely dominated

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:21.120
or to completely
dominate another player.

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:24.300
You want it to be such that
there at almost similar power

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:26.942
levels so it's very close
and they have a really

00:18:26.942 --> 00:18:28.150
interesting game in that way.

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:29.610
How do you solve that problem?

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:31.880
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
So for War Dragons

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:33.450
we have millions of players.

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.830
And every player is going
to have a different level.

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:36.330
Right?

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:39.290
If you just started right now,
you only have a few dragons.

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:42.640
Your base has only
a few buildings.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:45.690
But if you've been playing for
a year, as some of our players

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:47.810
have, you have a full base.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:50.737
You have a lot of dragons and
a lot of very strong dragons

00:18:50.737 --> 00:18:51.570
that you can battle.

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.694
So we don't want those two
people battling together

00:18:53.694 --> 00:18:55.640
because one is just
going to crush the other

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:57.690
and the other is not
going to win any battles.

00:18:57.690 --> 00:18:58.190
Right?

00:18:58.190 --> 00:18:59.564
So you don't want
that to happen.

00:18:59.564 --> 00:19:01.170
It's going to
frustrate your users.

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:04.300
So what we do is we
create basically buckets

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:10.180
of users with their
power level basically.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.100
So we calculate your power.

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:15.300
We put people that have
that relative power

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:16.760
with the same bucket.

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:20.250
And whenever we need to
fetch an opponent whenever

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:22.430
a client goes, hey, I
want to battle someone,

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:24.580
we can quickly see
what is your bucket.

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:26.650
We go to that
bucket in Datastore.

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:29.280
We fetch any of the
opponents that are there,

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:30.740
we have all the
data that we need

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.310
is in that bucket
in the Datastore

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:35.020
and we serve that to the client.

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:38.560
So it's a really efficient
way to have people battling

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:41.620
people that have
their same power

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:45.850
level without having to do
big queries in a Datastore

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:46.820
to search for people.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:48.510
So we already do that.

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:51.870
We do that in an
asynchronous way

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.410
so it doesn't take any
time for the users.

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:59.820
So whenever let's say a user
does a battle or upgrades

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:04.730
a building or gets a
new dragon, what we do

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:07.770
is we add a task
to our task queues.

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:10.180
And that task queue
is going to see, OK,

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:13.370
what is your power
level now and see if it

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:15.260
has to change your bucket.

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:17.420
So if it decides that it
has to change your bucket,

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:19.600
it simply takes you
out of one bucket,

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:20.880
puts you in another bucket.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:23.800
And now when you battle
for the next time,

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:26.530
it simply picks you
of your next bucket.

00:20:26.530 --> 00:20:28.640
So a really simple
solution to something that

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:33.820
would be really, really complex
in a normal database system.

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:37.180
So using a Datastore
and task queues just

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:38.710
became really simple for us.

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:41.900
Very easy and fast for
the end user, as well.

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:42.650
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:48.390
So I know you also
do special events

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:49.750
on occasion within your world.

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:51.410
I know you do a
conquer the world map

00:20:51.410 --> 00:20:54.970
where you have guilds of players
to fight against each other.

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:56.700
Tell us about what
happens there.

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:58.150
I know you have
to render a world

00:20:58.150 --> 00:21:01.147
map so all the people in the
game can see what's going on.

00:21:01.147 --> 00:21:02.980
What sort of problems
are you solving there?

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:03.938
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Sure.

00:21:03.938 --> 00:21:06.630
So conquer the world was an
event that we had for a weekend

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:11.420
where guilds-- which are groups
of players that band together

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:15.450
to serve as allies-- the
guilds would start everyone

00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:18.070
with their own territory
in a map such as this.

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:22.020
And throughout the weekend you
would battle your opponents

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:25.850
and gain or lose territory.

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:29.400
And so the goal was to have
as much territory as you have.

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:31.150
This is what happened
during that weekend.

00:21:31.150 --> 00:21:34.150
So you can see all the players--
all the guilds-- expanding

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:35.979
and contracting.

00:21:35.979 --> 00:21:37.770
There was a lot of
strategy involved there.

00:21:37.770 --> 00:21:39.986
People were trying to save
their dragons to the end

00:21:39.986 --> 00:21:41.400
so the could conquer more.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:43.470
So you see the green
team and the yellow team.

00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:44.450
MARK MANDEL: I see
the orange team seems

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:45.610
to be doing very, very well.

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:47.818
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: The orange
team and the green team.

00:21:47.818 --> 00:21:51.960
That was the final result
of that weekend for one

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:53.900
of our divisions.

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:56.120
So what we wanted is
we didn't want just

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.210
to have something where
you draw a number,

00:21:58.210 --> 00:22:02.370
you have like how many square
feet you have of territory.

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:05.200
We wanted users to have
a map, to have that sense

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.630
that they are attacking
and gaining territory.

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:09.250
So to doing that
on the client side

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:11.100
would be extremely
difficult for us.

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:13.772
So we decided to do
that on the server side.

00:22:13.772 --> 00:22:15.230
So on the server
side what happened

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:19.650
was we have task queues
going in periodically

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:21.320
we're doing that
every five minutes.

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:23.420
So every five minutes
those task queues

00:22:23.420 --> 00:22:26.040
would grab every data
from every battle

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:28.610
that everybody fought
during those five minutes,

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:34.920
recalculate territories, and
actually generate that image.

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:36.170
So this is for every division.

00:22:36.170 --> 00:22:38.480
Division is a
collection of guilds,

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:40.600
which is kind of like
matchmaking for guilds

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:42.816
for that level of guilds.

00:22:42.816 --> 00:22:44.190
So you can see
there that we have

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:48.560
those guilds which are actually
real names of divisions

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:49.780
that we have.

00:22:49.780 --> 00:22:52.870
And every task queue
is calculating that.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:54.310
One of the things
that we did here

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:59.920
which is a little bit different
than what task queues do,

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:02.270
whenever you add
something to a task queue,

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:05.300
if you add 100 things to a
task queue, Google App Engine

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:10.030
is going to try to run
that as fast as possible.

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:13.390
So if you have 100, if
you have like two servers,

00:23:13.390 --> 00:23:16.710
it's going to try to spin a
bunch of servers, execute that.

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:19.180
When you don't have anything,
it takes down your server.

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:21.540
So this would be
something what you

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:26.390
would see if we had those
task queues running with what

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:28.220
is called the default scaling.

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:30.420
So every five minutes
you would see a spike

00:23:30.420 --> 00:23:33.160
in the number of servers that
Google is spinning up for you.

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:34.170
Then it spins down.

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:35.270
You go almost to zero.

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:36.750
And then five
minutes later you're

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:38.860
spinning up a bunch of things.

00:23:38.860 --> 00:23:42.100
So this is very inefficient
from a computing standpoint

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:44.880
and from a cost
perspective, too.

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:48.110
So, for this case, because we
knew the number of divisions

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:49.980
that we had-- like the
number of divisions

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:51.438
doesn't change
during the weekend--

00:23:51.438 --> 00:23:53.550
we change that on
a periodic basis.

00:23:53.550 --> 00:23:55.972
But we know it's not going
to change during the event.

00:23:55.972 --> 00:23:58.430
And we know that we want to
render that every five minutes.

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:02.730
And we know rendering takes
an x amount of seconds.

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:05.090
What we do is we
use basic scaling.

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:10.240
So basic skating is you say,
don't spend more than x number

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:11.530
of servers for me.

00:24:11.530 --> 00:24:15.180
So when you do that, then you
see something more like that.

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:19.130
So you still add 100, 200
tasks in your task queue,

00:24:19.130 --> 00:24:21.130
but Google is not going
to spin up more servers.

00:24:21.130 --> 00:24:23.224
It might spin up one, maybe not.

00:24:23.224 --> 00:24:25.390
But you're going to see
something way more constant,

00:24:25.390 --> 00:24:28.330
which is great for
your cost and is also

00:24:28.330 --> 00:24:31.560
great for your computing
because computers don't have

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:33.594
to start and stop all the time.

00:24:33.594 --> 00:24:35.760
So this is one of the things
that we did differently

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:39.162
than the default scaling that
everybody uses out there.

00:24:39.162 --> 00:24:40.620
MARK MANDEL: So
this is really cool

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:43.582
because you knew exactly
how many divisions you had,

00:24:43.582 --> 00:24:45.290
and you knew that
wasn't going to change.

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:47.130
You were able to take
advantage of that to get more

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:48.250
efficiency out of your system.

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:48.540
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:50.920
Whenever you have that
fixed computing power

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:53.650
that you know that's what
you need, using basic

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:57.010
is a better use of
your computing power.

00:24:57.010 --> 00:24:57.760
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:01.840
Did I go forward?

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:04.160
I believe you also use
memcache and some other things

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:05.362
as well in that aspect?

00:25:05.362 --> 00:25:06.320
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:09.150
Some other improvements
that we did

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:13.330
to conquer the world map using
some of the Google App Engine

00:25:13.330 --> 00:25:13.830
features.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:16.500
So we used memcache
to cache the metadata.

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:17.560
So we had some metadata.

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:19.500
Like, for example,
where is your capitals?

00:25:19.500 --> 00:25:21.810
So the client would
render the name

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:23.780
of the guild inside of the map.

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:25.680
So we had to have
where's that going to be

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:26.970
rendered on the client side?

00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:28.300
So we had some metadata.

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:31.260
And we had a memcache
to catch that,

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:33.770
because it's really
fast for the client

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:35.770
to grab that information.

00:25:35.770 --> 00:25:37.720
We also used something
called Google Edge

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:41.760
Cache which it's basically a
caching of your information.

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:44.010
So whenever we
generate that image,

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:46.510
Google asks us for that image.

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:48.940
We say this image can be
cached for five minutes.

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:52.380
So then we don't use that
computing power anymore.

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:53.990
Google Edge Cache
just takes care,

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:57.150
it knows that that image can
be stored for five minutes

00:25:57.150 --> 00:25:58.150
and it just serves that.

00:25:58.150 --> 00:26:01.220
So it's something that also
saves us money and makes

00:26:01.220 --> 00:26:03.890
it way faster for the client
to get that image because you

00:26:03.890 --> 00:26:06.580
don't have to hit a
computer to grab that image

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:08.360
and return that for you.

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:10.420
It returns from Edge
Cache almost immediately.

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:12.650
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:26:12.650 --> 00:26:15.420
So I know you also have a
leaderboard system so players

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:18.660
can rank themselves or be ranked
within a whole subset of all

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:19.225
the players.

00:26:19.225 --> 00:26:21.100
I know that's kind of
an interesting problem.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:22.690
Do you want to
describe the problem?

00:26:22.690 --> 00:26:23.510
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: So,
yeah, leaderboards

00:26:23.510 --> 00:26:24.635
are an interesting problem.

00:26:24.635 --> 00:26:26.760
They seem simple
in the beginning,

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:28.960
but then you start
to see that it's

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:30.510
more complicated than it seems.

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:32.210
So leaderboards,
they basically need

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:34.020
to answer those three
questions for you

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:35.860
in a very efficient way.

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:37.500
So when a leaderboard,
I need to know

00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:39.350
what is my position
in a leaderboard.

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:40.714
It needs to answer that fast.

00:26:40.714 --> 00:26:42.880
And then it needs to answer
what are the people that

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:45.000
are around me, so I
can see like people

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:47.180
that I want to attack
later so I can jump up

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:49.200
on the leaderboards.

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:52.520
And it also needs to answer
like who are the people that

00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:54.660
are in position 100 to 120.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:58.040
If our users are paginating,
if they're scrolling,

00:26:58.040 --> 00:26:59.220
you need to grab more data.

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:02.480
You need to be able to grab
positions at 100 to 120

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:04.080
really fast.

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:05.990
So it seems easy, right?

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:07.550
But as you guys
are going to see,

00:27:07.550 --> 00:27:11.130
databases are not really suited
to answer those questions.

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:13.500
So let's see a little bit why.

00:27:13.500 --> 00:27:15.182
So this is an example.

00:27:15.182 --> 00:27:16.890
Actually this was one
of the leaderboards

00:27:16.890 --> 00:27:20.850
that we had a few days
ago that I grabbed.

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:22.679
So let's say that you
have this database

00:27:22.679 --> 00:27:23.720
and say, OK, that's easy.

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:25.886
I'm just going to create a
database with player name

00:27:25.886 --> 00:27:26.850
and score.

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:29.900
I create indices for the
player name and the score

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:33.670
so it's easy for me to say,
OK, I want to see the Masse--

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:35.362
or I don't know how
that's pronounced.

00:27:35.362 --> 00:27:36.820
I don't know why
I chose that name.

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:38.403
There a better names
for me to choose.

00:27:38.403 --> 00:27:40.000
But OK.

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:42.930
So that Masse guy there, you
want to know its position,

00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:44.470
it's easy for you
to check Masse.

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:47.646
But it's not easy to
know the position, right?

00:27:47.646 --> 00:27:49.020
Because databases,
they basically

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:52.840
have to count everybody that
comes before that person

00:27:52.840 --> 00:27:53.740
to know the position.

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:55.110
There's no position there.

00:27:55.110 --> 00:27:57.520
So then you say, easy,
just add a position there.

00:27:57.520 --> 00:27:58.580
Why not?

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:01.680
So you add up a column for
position and now you know.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:03.750
Oh, Masse is number nine.

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:04.250
Easy.

00:28:04.250 --> 00:28:05.570
Problem solved.

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:09.790
Not so much because what
happens when Masse wins a battle

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:11.930
and jumps up on the leaderboard?

00:28:11.930 --> 00:28:14.600
Now you have this
weird thing here

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:16.802
where Masse is
actually number four,

00:28:16.802 --> 00:28:19.260
but the position says number
nine, because the whole record

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:20.070
moves up.

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:22.360
You can't move partially.

00:28:22.360 --> 00:28:25.390
So now you imagine already
what you have to do here.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:27.980
You have to go and change
a bunch of those records.

00:28:27.980 --> 00:28:31.680
You have to change all the
records between four and nine

00:28:31.680 --> 00:28:32.760
there.

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:34.550
And that's a very
costly thing that you're

00:28:34.550 --> 00:28:36.924
changing a bunch of records
that potentially other people

00:28:36.924 --> 00:28:37.644
are changing.

00:28:37.644 --> 00:28:38.810
So it's not an easy problem.

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:40.740
It's not as easy as it looks.

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:42.840
So when we started
building War Dragons

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:44.470
we started looking
for solutions.

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:46.450
OK, people had that
problem before.

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:47.586
Games have been out there.

00:28:47.586 --> 00:28:48.960
Competitions have
been out there.

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:50.860
People have had
that problem before.

00:28:50.860 --> 00:28:53.590
And, lo and behold,
we found some project

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:55.970
that Google did for
Google Code Jam.

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:58.340
Because Google Code Jam
is a competition of code,

00:28:58.340 --> 00:29:01.010
they had a leaderboard of sorts.

00:29:01.010 --> 00:29:04.360
And they released that
project as open source

00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:06.270
for Google App
Engine, because that's

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:09.370
how they run Google Code Jam.

00:29:09.370 --> 00:29:11.880
So, Mark, why don't
you explain to us how

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:13.160
about that solved our problem?

00:29:13.160 --> 00:29:14.118
MARK MANDEL: Certainly.

00:29:14.118 --> 00:29:16.410
So I had a look at
this code and I'm

00:29:16.410 --> 00:29:18.090
going to explain
it fairly simply.

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:21.250
There's a link to the blog post,
which has a link to the code.

00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:24.590
So if you want to go deeper
into it, more than happy to.

00:29:24.590 --> 00:29:27.360
Essentially what it does
is it uses Datastore

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:30.060
to build a data structure
inside of it that's

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:32.310
able to answer these sort
of leaderboard questions

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:33.190
very simply.

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:34.990
What it actually
builds is a tree.

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:37.790
So the first thing you do
when using this code is you

00:29:37.790 --> 00:29:40.230
select your range of
what your score could be.

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:42.680
Here I've selected an
arbitrary range of zero

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:46.370
to 10,000 as that's what my
score is going to land in to.

00:29:46.370 --> 00:29:48.400
Then inside that
tree we're going

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:51.210
to pick essentially an arbitrary
number of nodes underneath it.

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:53.550
Here we're going to just
use three for simplicity.

00:29:53.550 --> 00:29:55.270
And we're going to
split up those ranges

00:29:55.270 --> 00:29:56.780
into each of those thirds.

00:29:56.780 --> 00:30:00.170
So you can see like 0 to
3,000, 3,000 to 6,000,

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:01.310
so on and so forth.

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:04.470
And then so on and so forth
down the tree as we go.

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:05.640
And that's great.

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:07.610
And then what happens
is when we have

00:30:07.610 --> 00:30:09.010
a score come in--
so someone gets

00:30:09.010 --> 00:30:12.000
their score set-- it goes
in the tree in that range

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:13.030
that they fit.

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:13.870
That's cool.

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:15.780
But what we actually
do at each node

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:18.200
is we count up
how many scores we

00:30:18.200 --> 00:30:21.140
have for each of the
children in a parent node.

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:23.810
So if we had 100 scores, it
would look something like this.

00:30:23.810 --> 00:30:26.576
At the top we have 50, 25, 25.

00:30:26.576 --> 00:30:27.950
If we continued
down the left, we

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:29.491
would add up all of
those to then 50.

00:30:29.491 --> 00:30:33.710
So 10, 25, 15, so on and
so forth down the tree.

00:30:33.710 --> 00:30:35.860
This is actually really
powerful for leaderboards.

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:38.110
If we want to ask
questions like, hey,

00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:40.400
how many scores do
I have in total?

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:42.870
All I need to do is count
up the number of children

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:45.695
that I would have in the top
node, and that would be 100.

00:30:45.695 --> 00:30:47.070
If I wanted to do
something like,

00:30:47.070 --> 00:30:50.190
hey, I want to go find
the top 10 scores,

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:52.850
all I need to do is go down
the left side of my tree

00:30:52.850 --> 00:30:55.650
until I've counted up
until I have 10 results.

00:30:55.650 --> 00:30:59.470
If I had a score
that was 2,222 and I

00:30:59.470 --> 00:31:01.240
wanted to know what
position that was,

00:31:01.240 --> 00:31:03.099
I could just count to
the left of that tree,

00:31:03.099 --> 00:31:05.140
and potentially upward,
depending on my position,

00:31:05.140 --> 00:31:06.000
as well.

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:07.680
So we can answer these
sort of questions

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:09.770
really easily using
this data structure.

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:13.300
But, like I said, the notes
for this and the URL for this

00:31:13.300 --> 00:31:14.800
are all at the very
end of the talk.

00:31:14.800 --> 00:31:16.000
So if you want to dig
this into further,

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:18.336
this is a very simple
explanation of how it works.

00:31:18.336 --> 00:31:19.960
But it does solve
this sort of problem.

00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:27.460
I believe also App Engine, for
you, you use it in development,

00:31:27.460 --> 00:31:29.209
you use it in a whole
bunch of other ways.

00:31:29.209 --> 00:31:30.470
You love it that much.

00:31:30.470 --> 00:31:31.647
Tell us about that.

00:31:31.647 --> 00:31:33.230
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
So one of the things

00:31:33.230 --> 00:31:35.660
that we do that is the classic
way of using versioning,

00:31:35.660 --> 00:31:38.010
we use versioning a lot
to develop new features.

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:41.930
So whenever a developer needs
to develop something else,

00:31:41.930 --> 00:31:43.820
every developer has
their own version

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:45.510
of the server running out there.

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:46.680
It's really easy to deploy.

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:48.900
There's a desktop
app, press a button,

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:50.300
it deploys that for you.

00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:52.580
And then you can test
your features there.

00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:55.750
And whenever that feature
is properly approved,

00:31:55.750 --> 00:31:57.300
you can then put it on master.

00:31:57.300 --> 00:32:00.700
And then we have a process that
deploys that to our production

00:32:00.700 --> 00:32:02.100
servers all the time.

00:32:02.100 --> 00:32:03.700
So every developer
has their own.

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:05.610
Nobody steps on anybody's toes.

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:06.830
Classic use of that.

00:32:06.830 --> 00:32:08.350
We do that all the time.

00:32:08.350 --> 00:32:11.990
Another thing that we do a
lot is we like GAE so much

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:13.900
and it's is so easy
to develop for that,

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:18.050
that we do our own internal
tools for that using Google App

00:32:18.050 --> 00:32:18.760
Engine.

00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:20.750
So at some point War
Dragons grew a lot.

00:32:20.750 --> 00:32:22.970
It was our largest team to date.

00:32:22.970 --> 00:32:25.880
We had more engineers than
any other team at Pocket Gems

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:27.050
had before.

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:29.390
So we had to step up
our game in code reviews

00:32:29.390 --> 00:32:32.340
and making sure that our
quality of code was good.

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:34.220
And we didn't have
any tools out there

00:32:34.220 --> 00:32:36.710
that would serve us
the way we wanted to,

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:38.160
so we just wrote our own.

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:40.670
Like one of our developers
worked for a little bit less

00:32:40.670 --> 00:32:44.024
than a week, wrote a cold
review portal for us,

00:32:44.024 --> 00:32:45.190
and now we use all the time.

00:32:45.190 --> 00:32:49.790
The whole company now uses
that code review portal.

00:32:49.790 --> 00:32:51.580
We started adding
a lot of process.

00:32:51.580 --> 00:32:54.320
And we all know that
developers hate process.

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:56.460
So what I did was
I built a robot

00:32:56.460 --> 00:32:58.805
using Google App Engine
that just goes and does

00:32:58.805 --> 00:32:59.750
the process for us.

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:01.125
We don't have to
care about that.

00:33:01.125 --> 00:33:04.490
We just do a few things on
GitHub or on our ticketing

00:33:04.490 --> 00:33:08.170
system, and the robot does
all the process things for us.

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:12.040
So I just wrote that in a few
days, rolled up a new server.

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:12.550
It's there.

00:33:12.550 --> 00:33:13.980
We have a new server running.

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:15.040
Really, really easy.

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:18.052
We use that all the time for
our development tools, as well.

00:33:18.052 --> 00:33:19.010
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:20.920
So you love it so much for
building your production apps,

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:22.480
you use it internally, as well.

00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:23.620
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: I even
have some of my own.

00:33:23.620 --> 00:33:25.430
Like I like it so
much that, oh, I

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:26.880
can do that for my own servers.

00:33:26.880 --> 00:33:29.410
I was building some
little electronics thing,

00:33:29.410 --> 00:33:31.430
and I needed a server
to serve information.

00:33:31.430 --> 00:33:33.175
Oh, I'll just do it
in Google App Engine.

00:33:33.175 --> 00:33:35.320
It's free for very small use.

00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:37.935
So I just wrote my
own and deployed it

00:33:37.935 --> 00:33:39.440
on my personal account.

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:40.230
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:33:40.230 --> 00:33:41.150
OK.

00:33:41.150 --> 00:33:44.890
So it can't all be
sugar and spice.

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:48.780
There had to have been some
challenges and some problems.

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:50.480
We should probably
have a look at those

00:33:50.480 --> 00:33:54.400
and see possibly what
dragons you had to slay.

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:55.975
Terrible joke.

00:33:55.975 --> 00:33:57.350
Let's have a look
at some of that

00:33:57.350 --> 00:33:59.660
and so people here can
learn from the lessons

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:01.992
you learned while you
were building War Dragons.

00:34:01.992 --> 00:34:02.950
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Sure.

00:34:02.950 --> 00:34:04.700
So one of the
problems that we faced

00:34:04.700 --> 00:34:08.389
is that War Dragons generates
a lot of what we call events.

00:34:08.389 --> 00:34:11.120
So events are various things
that happen inside the game

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:12.909
that we can be
interested in to know

00:34:12.909 --> 00:34:16.219
how people are using the game,
what time of the day people

00:34:16.219 --> 00:34:16.929
play more.

00:34:16.929 --> 00:34:19.139
So there are a lot of things
that happen in the game

00:34:19.139 --> 00:34:22.630
that we save as events and then
we send that to our servers

00:34:22.630 --> 00:34:24.929
so we can analyze
that data later.

00:34:24.929 --> 00:34:28.210
But, as you can imagine, we
have like millions and millions

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:32.889
of lines of events coming in
all the time from our users.

00:34:32.889 --> 00:34:37.230
And the way that the
Datastore costs are made,

00:34:37.230 --> 00:34:40.580
you are charged by
operations in your Datastore.

00:34:40.580 --> 00:34:43.500
So if you're saving one
event in your Datastore,

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:45.770
that is one operation.

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:48.590
If you're saving 100
events in the same table

00:34:48.590 --> 00:34:52.130
in the Datastore, that
is also one event.

00:34:52.130 --> 00:34:54.620
So we needed to figure
out a way that we

00:34:54.620 --> 00:34:56.080
could save money on that.

00:34:56.080 --> 00:34:59.270
Like instead of getting
information from a user,

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:01.750
we have 100 events there
and doing 100 operations

00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:03.860
because they all go
to different tables,

00:35:03.860 --> 00:35:07.470
we needed to figure out a way
to do that to-- I'm sorry,

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:10.650
Mark-- but to reduce a
little bit of our costs.

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:16.470
So let me tell you how we
did that using GAE zone

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:17.690
infrastructure.

00:35:17.690 --> 00:35:20.290
So what we did was we
used memcache for this

00:35:20.290 --> 00:35:22.650
because memcache is really
well suited for this problem.

00:35:22.650 --> 00:35:24.720
It's very fast.

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:27.360
So what we did was
basically we create a bunch

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:31.710
of records in our memcache
that receive the events

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:33.750
from the players.

00:35:33.750 --> 00:35:37.140
So these particular
records in the Datastore,

00:35:37.140 --> 00:35:40.744
they just accumulate a bunch
of events of different types.

00:35:40.744 --> 00:35:41.410
They don't care.

00:35:41.410 --> 00:35:44.140
They just start to get
that information there.

00:35:44.140 --> 00:35:47.060
At some point, memcache
has a size limit.

00:35:47.060 --> 00:35:48.650
So at some point you
reach that limit.

00:35:48.650 --> 00:35:50.280
When we reach that
limit, what we do

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:53.600
is we fire up a task queue.

00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:56.770
That task queue now
grabs all the events

00:35:56.770 --> 00:36:01.730
from that particular
record that is full

00:36:01.730 --> 00:36:06.950
and it starts to sort into
a second level of records

00:36:06.950 --> 00:36:09.060
in memcache, which
are, in this case,

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:10.610
particular to every event.

00:36:10.610 --> 00:36:12.790
So if it's an event type
a, it goes to that record.

00:36:12.790 --> 00:36:15.090
If it's an event type b,
it goes to that record,

00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:16.600
and so on and so forth.

00:36:16.600 --> 00:36:18.160
And when that
record is empty, it

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:20.810
can be reused by other clients.

00:36:20.810 --> 00:36:24.310
At the same time, those
other second level records

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:26.000
are also going to become full.

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:31.780
When they become full, we fire
off another task in task queue

00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:34.480
and now they put that
into the Datastore.

00:36:34.480 --> 00:36:38.110
So now we turn what
could be thousands

00:36:38.110 --> 00:36:41.010
of events that could be
thousands of operations,

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:43.570
now they do only one
operation because now they're

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:47.630
sorted by their table and they
go straight to that table.

00:36:47.630 --> 00:36:51.960
And then they can be reused
for another operation.

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:53.910
But with that, we
were able to reduce

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:55.540
millions and millions
of operations

00:36:55.540 --> 00:36:58.202
to a few operations per minute.

00:36:58.202 --> 00:36:59.160
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:01.180
So you were able to
use memcache to really

00:37:01.180 --> 00:37:02.930
make that an efficient
and faster process.

00:37:02.930 --> 00:37:04.310
And, as we've seen
before, you're

00:37:04.310 --> 00:37:06.142
using memcache all over
the place, as well.

00:37:06.142 --> 00:37:07.100
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:37:07.100 --> 00:37:08.390
MARK MANDEL: Great.

00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:10.070
So one other issue
I know you faced

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:12.840
is Datastore data
contention issues.

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:15.710
For those that
aren't familiar, one

00:37:15.710 --> 00:37:18.370
of the issues with
Datastore or possibly one

00:37:18.370 --> 00:37:20.020
of the challenges
with Datastore is

00:37:20.020 --> 00:37:21.980
there are some hard
limits around how fast you

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:25.210
can write or change
data on a single entity.

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:27.410
So if you have two
processes operating

00:37:27.410 --> 00:37:29.077
on a single entity
within Datastore

00:37:29.077 --> 00:37:30.910
at a given point in
time, it can turn around

00:37:30.910 --> 00:37:32.450
and be like, whoa, slow down.

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:34.614
I'd like you to stop
hitting me so fast.

00:37:34.614 --> 00:37:36.030
I know that was a
problem for you.

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:38.620
You want to talk about that and
how you ended up solving it?

00:37:38.620 --> 00:37:38.870
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:37:38.870 --> 00:37:40.790
Data contention was
a problem for us.

00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:43.440
So you can imagine that we
have some operations that

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:44.900
touch a lot of things.

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:47.000
So I'm going to give the
example of data reports.

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:49.030
So whenever you
finish off a battle,

00:37:49.030 --> 00:37:51.560
you send that data
report to our servers.

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:53.480
And our servers need
to do a lot of stuff.

00:37:53.480 --> 00:37:58.090
So it needs to award the loot
that you got from that battle.

00:37:58.090 --> 00:38:02.040
At the same time it
needs to remove resources

00:38:02.040 --> 00:38:03.264
from your opponent's record.

00:38:03.264 --> 00:38:04.930
It needs to award you
metals so that you

00:38:04.930 --> 00:38:06.800
can go up on the leaderboards.

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:09.613
So all that touches one record--
touches two records actually

00:38:09.613 --> 00:38:10.589
in the players.

00:38:10.589 --> 00:38:12.380
You have to actually
add to the war results

00:38:12.380 --> 00:38:13.900
if that battle is part of a war.

00:38:13.900 --> 00:38:16.210
You have to go and
add the war results.

00:38:16.210 --> 00:38:17.860
You have to change
your leaderboards.

00:38:17.860 --> 00:38:19.710
You have to change your
matchmaking bucket.

00:38:19.710 --> 00:38:24.850
And then after you did all that,
now you respond to the user.

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:26.904
So one problem is
there's a lot of time

00:38:26.904 --> 00:38:28.570
that the user isl not
getting a response

00:38:28.570 --> 00:38:32.110
and maybe your game is
waiting on a waiting screen.

00:38:32.110 --> 00:38:34.080
So that's not a good
user experience.

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:36.360
And the other problem
is that with this you

00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:38.890
imagine with millions of users
doing that, a lot of them

00:38:38.890 --> 00:38:40.960
might be hitting
the same record.

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:45.360
So if two people from the
same guild are battling a war,

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:47.670
they all are going to
hit the same war record.

00:38:47.670 --> 00:38:49.850
And when that happens
you get data contention.

00:38:49.850 --> 00:38:53.820
One client needs to wait
for the other one to finish.

00:38:53.820 --> 00:38:57.460
And if they wait too
long, this operation

00:38:57.460 --> 00:38:59.650
is going to fail completely.

00:38:59.650 --> 00:39:01.820
So not a good solution.

00:39:01.820 --> 00:39:05.770
So what we did was, again, using
all the resources for Google

00:39:05.770 --> 00:39:08.130
App Engine, we broke that down.

00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:12.730
So what we did was we do the
least amount of possible work

00:39:12.730 --> 00:39:15.630
that we need to give a
response to the user.

00:39:15.630 --> 00:39:17.130
So the user at this
point only needs

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:20.680
to know how much loot
you've got from that battle.

00:39:20.680 --> 00:39:21.210
That's it.

00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:22.490
So we do that.

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:25.020
And then we add a
task queue what's

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:26.242
called a transactional task.

00:39:26.242 --> 00:39:28.450
It's kind of a task queue
but has special properties,

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:30.150
which is really nice.

00:39:30.150 --> 00:39:33.470
We, at that point,
the award just

00:39:33.470 --> 00:39:35.680
goes to one record over there.

00:39:35.680 --> 00:39:38.180
Then we return to the
user-- so really fast.

00:39:38.180 --> 00:39:41.580
And then we add a task
to the task queue.

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:44.060
That task is going to
do all those things

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:45.484
that we were doing before.

00:39:45.484 --> 00:39:47.150
So it's still going
to remove resources.

00:39:47.150 --> 00:39:49.379
It's still going
to give you metals.

00:39:49.379 --> 00:39:51.170
It's still going to
add to the war results.

00:39:51.170 --> 00:39:52.540
Still going to
change leaderboards.

00:39:52.540 --> 00:39:54.500
Still going to change
your matchmaking bucket.

00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:55.600
All of that.

00:39:55.600 --> 00:39:58.290
And then you are going
to ask, but can't that

00:39:58.290 --> 00:40:00.100
run into data
contention, as well?

00:40:00.100 --> 00:40:02.710
You're doing a lot of
stuff into one operation.

00:40:02.710 --> 00:40:03.750
And yes, you are right.

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:04.570
Thank you.

00:40:04.570 --> 00:40:05.990
You're a very smart
audience here.

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:07.950
So that happens.

00:40:07.950 --> 00:40:11.920
But when that happens, it's an
awesome feature of task queues

00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:14.350
is that they just retry.

00:40:14.350 --> 00:40:16.090
So if there's any
data convention there

00:40:16.090 --> 00:40:19.210
and your operation fails, it
just goes back to the queue

00:40:19.210 --> 00:40:22.150
and after awhile
it just retries.

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:24.140
So when it retries,
at some point

00:40:24.140 --> 00:40:26.930
it's not going to phase data
contention, and at some point

00:40:26.930 --> 00:40:28.260
it will commit all of that.

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:31.420
It takes a while, but it
doesn't change the experience

00:40:31.420 --> 00:40:33.000
in any way for our players.

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:34.690
Those operations
don't change anything

00:40:34.690 --> 00:40:36.710
in a meaningful way
for our players.

00:40:36.710 --> 00:40:38.260
So they can be done
asynchronously.

00:40:38.260 --> 00:40:39.540
They can take a while.

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:40.870
They can be retried.

00:40:40.870 --> 00:40:44.622
So that's a really, really
nice feature of task queues.

00:40:44.622 --> 00:40:45.580
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:40:45.580 --> 00:40:47.413
So you're able to take
advantage of the fact

00:40:47.413 --> 00:40:50.170
that you have a lot of control
over how task queues can retry,

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:53.234
exponential back off, number
of retries, number of days,

00:40:53.234 --> 00:40:55.150
et cetera, et cetera,
and so you can make sure

00:40:55.150 --> 00:40:56.260
that this always happens.

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:57.440
It's just a question
of how long.

00:40:57.440 --> 00:40:57.950
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:40:57.950 --> 00:40:58.670
Task queues are awesome.

00:40:58.670 --> 00:40:59.990
If you have bug in
your code, you can just

00:40:59.990 --> 00:41:01.420
change that code and redeploy.

00:41:01.420 --> 00:41:05.460
All your operations that
failed re-run on that new code.

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:08.190
So you can fix code on the fly.

00:41:08.190 --> 00:41:10.210
And you can see the
size of your queues.

00:41:10.210 --> 00:41:10.760
It's amazing.

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:11.670
It's really awesome.

00:41:11.670 --> 00:41:14.420
We have some queues from
our old games there.

00:41:14.420 --> 00:41:16.860
Just a fun fact,
we have tasks that

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:18.297
have been running
for two or three

00:41:18.297 --> 00:41:19.880
years because we
have buggy code there

00:41:19.880 --> 00:41:21.960
and because it's an
old game we don't care.

00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:23.850
We just let those
things run there.

00:41:23.850 --> 00:41:26.210
And they've been running for
like three or four years.

00:41:26.210 --> 00:41:27.800
We have old tasks like that.

00:41:27.800 --> 00:41:28.681
It's relentless.

00:41:28.681 --> 00:41:29.430
MARK MANDEL: Fine.

00:41:29.430 --> 00:41:30.390
Cool.

00:41:30.390 --> 00:41:34.130
All right, so I'm sure
your code is perfect.

00:41:34.130 --> 00:41:36.730
Mine often has some issues.

00:41:36.730 --> 00:41:39.470
I know App Engine gives you
log aggregation by default.

00:41:39.470 --> 00:41:40.730
But how do you use that?

00:41:40.730 --> 00:41:42.438
I know you've got some
nice ways that you

00:41:42.438 --> 00:41:43.820
use that with War Dragons.

00:41:43.820 --> 00:41:44.000
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:46.370
So the log aggregation
that we get out of the box,

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:47.330
it's really useful.

00:41:47.330 --> 00:41:48.450
It's really easy to use.

00:41:48.450 --> 00:41:50.410
So this is just
the default console

00:41:50.410 --> 00:41:53.100
log you can access
for Google App Engine.

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:55.170
And you have a
search box over there

00:41:55.170 --> 00:41:56.840
at the top that
you can just search

00:41:56.840 --> 00:42:00.290
for anything in your
request or in your headers.

00:42:00.290 --> 00:42:01.760
So in our case,
for example, we use

00:42:01.760 --> 00:42:05.100
that a lot to track down
problems that users are having.

00:42:05.100 --> 00:42:06.860
Every user has a
unique ID, which

00:42:06.860 --> 00:42:09.880
is a hash like ID, that
is really easy to search

00:42:09.880 --> 00:42:13.570
for because there's
you're never going to find

00:42:13.570 --> 00:42:15.020
another one in your request.

00:42:15.020 --> 00:42:16.780
So whenever we need
to know what requests

00:42:16.780 --> 00:42:19.550
are failing for a certain
user we just go there.

00:42:19.550 --> 00:42:22.250
We search for their ID.

00:42:22.250 --> 00:42:25.610
It comes up with
all the results,

00:42:25.610 --> 00:42:26.760
and then we can dig in.

00:42:26.760 --> 00:42:30.380
We can go in and see the
metadata, the proto payload,

00:42:30.380 --> 00:42:34.660
we can see all the git
requests parameters.

00:42:34.660 --> 00:42:37.470
You can add logging
statements to your code

00:42:37.470 --> 00:42:40.355
and they will also appear here
as the logging statements.

00:42:40.355 --> 00:42:41.980
If there was an
exception in your code,

00:42:41.980 --> 00:42:43.530
you're going to
see that exception.

00:42:43.530 --> 00:42:45.240
So it's a really,
really easy to use

00:42:45.240 --> 00:42:48.890
tool that we use all the time
that is right out of the box.

00:42:48.890 --> 00:42:51.020
We didn't have to do
anything to get this.

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:52.390
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:42:52.390 --> 00:42:53.800
All right.

00:42:53.800 --> 00:42:54.440
Great.

00:42:54.440 --> 00:42:58.200
So we've been talking today
about running games at scale.

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:00.140
